{
  "title": "18: al-Kahf الكهف",
  "code": "012",
  "data": "بِسْمِ اللهِ الرَّحْمٰنِ الرَّحِيْمِ\nBISMIL LAAHIR RAHMAANIR RAHEEM\nIn the Name of Allah, the All-beneficent, the All-merciful\nاَ لْحَمْدُ لِلّٰهِ الَّذِىْۤ اَنْزَلَ عَلٰى عَبْدِهِ الْكِتٰبَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَلْ لَّهٗ عِوَجًا  ؕ‏(1)\n\nAll praise belongs to Allah, who has sent down the Book to His servant and did not let any crookedness be in it,\nقَيِّمًا لِّيُنْذِرَ بَاْسًا شَدِيْدًا مِّنْ لَّدُنْهُ وَيُبَشِّرَ الْمُؤْمِنِيْنَ الَّذِيْنَ يَعْمَلُوْنَ الصّٰلِحٰتِ اَنَّ لَهُمْ اَجْرًا حَسَنًا ۙ‏(2)\n\n[a Book] upright, to warn of a severe punishment from Him, and to give good news to the faithful who do righteous deeds, that there shall be for them a good reward,\nمّٰكِثِيْنَ فِيْهِ اَبَدًا ۙ‏(3)\n\nto abide in it forever,\nوَّيُنْذِرَ الَّذِيْنَ قَالُوا اتَّخَذَ اللّٰهُ وَلَدًا‏(4)\n\nand to warn those who say, ‘Allah has taken a son.’\nمَا لَهُمْ بِهٖ مِنْ عِلْمٍ وَّلَا لِاٰبَآئِهِمْ‌ؕ كَبُرَتْ كَلِمَةً تَخْرُجُ مِنْ اَفْوَاهِهِمْ‌ؕ اِنْ يَّقُوْلُوْنَ اِلَّا كَذِبًا‏(5)\n\nThey do not have any knowledge of that, nor did their fathers. Monstrous is the utterance that comes out of their mouths, and they say nothing but a lie.\nفَلَعَلَّكَ بَاخِعٌ نَّفْسَكَ عَلٰٓى اٰثَارِهِمْ اِنْ لَّمْ يُؤْمِنُوْا بِهٰذَا الْحَدِيْثِ اَسَفًا‏(6)\n\nYou are liable to imperil your life out of grief for their sake, if they should not believe this discourse.\nاِنَّا جَعَلْنَا مَا عَلَى الْاَرْضِ زِيْنَةً لَّهَا لِنَبْلُوَهُمْ اَ يُّهُمْ اَحْسَنُ عَمَلًا‏(7)\n\nIndeed We have made whatever is on the earth an adornment for it that We may test them [to see] which of them is best in conduct.\nوَاِنَّا لَجٰعِلُوْنَ مَا عَلَيْهَا صَعِيْدًا جُرُزًا ؕ‏(8)\n\nAnd indeed We will turn whatever is on it into a barren plain.\nاَمْ حَسِبْتَ اَنَّ اَصْحٰبَ الْكَهْفِ وَالرَّقِيْمِۙ كَانُوْا مِنْ اٰيٰتِنَا عَجَبًا‏(9)\n\nDo you suppose that the Companions of the Cave and the Inscription were among Our wonderful signs?\nاِذْ اَوَى الْفِتْيَةُ اِلَى الْكَهْفِ فَقَالُوْا رَبَّنَاۤ اٰتِنَا مِنْ لَّدُنْكَ رَحْمَةً وَّهَيِّئْ لَنَا مِنْ اَمْرِنَا رَشَدًا‏(10)\n\nWhen the youths took refuge in the Cave, they said, ‘Our Lord! Grant us a mercy from Yourself, and help us on to rectitude in our affair.’\nفَضَرَبْنَا عَلٰٓى اٰذَانِهِمْ فِى الْكَهْفِ سِنِيْنَ عَدَدًا ۙ‏(11)\n\nSo We put them to sleep in the Cave for several years.\nثُمَّ بَعَثْنٰهُمْ لِنَعْلَمَ اَىُّ الْحِزْبَيْنِ اَحْصٰى لِمَا لَبِثُوْۤا اَمَدًا‏(12)\n\nThen We aroused them that We might know which of the two groups better reckoned the period they had stayed.\nنَحْنُ نَقُصُّ عَلَيْكَ نَبَاَهُمْ بِالْحَقِّ‌ؕ اِنَّهُمْ فِتْيَةٌ اٰمَنُوْا بِرَبِّهِمْ وَزِدْنٰهُمْ هُدًى‌ۖ‏(13)\n\nWe relate to you their account in truth. They were indeed youths who had faith in their Lord, and We had enhanced them in guidance,\nوَّرَبَطْنَا عَلٰى قُلُوْبِهِمْ اِذْ قَامُوْا فَقَالُوْا رَبُّنَا رَبُّ السَّمٰوٰتِ وَالْاَرْضِ لَنْ نَّدْعُوَا۫ مِنْ دُوْنِهٖۤ اِلٰهًا‌ لَّقَدْ قُلْنَاۤ اِذًا شَطَطًا‏(14)\n\nand fortified their hearts, when they stood up and said, ‘Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will never invoke any god besides Him, for then we shall certainly have said an atrocious lie.\nهٰٓؤُلَاۤءِ قَوْمُنَا اتَّخَذُوْا مِنْ دُوْنِهٖۤ اٰلِهَةً‌ ؕ لَوْ لَا يَاْتُوْنَ عَلَيْهِمْ بِسُلْطٰنٍۢ بَيِّنٍ‌ ؕ فَمَنْ اَظْلَمُ مِمَّنِ افْتَرٰى عَلَى اللّٰهِ كَذِبًا ؕ‏(15)\n\nThese—our people—have taken gods besides Him. Why do they not bring any clear authority touching them? So who is a greater wrongdoer than he who fabricates a lie against Allah?\nوَاِذِ اعْتَزَلْتُمُوْهُمْ وَمَا يَعْبُدُوْنَ اِلَّا اللّٰهَ فَاْوٗۤا اِلَى الْكَهْفِ يَنْشُرْ لَكُمْ رَبُّكُمْ مِّنْ رَّحْمَتِهٖ وَيُهَيِّئْ لَكُمْ مِّنْ اَمْرِكُمْ مِّرْفَقًا‏(16)\n\nWhen you have dissociated yourselves from them and from what they worship except Allah, then take refuge in the Cave. Your Lord will unfold His mercy for you, and He will help you on to ease in your affair.’\nوَتَرَى الشَّمْسَ اِذَا طَلَعَتْ تَّزٰوَرُ عَنْ كَهْفِهِمْ ذَاتَ الْيَمِيْنِ وَاِذَا غَرَبَتْ تَّقْرِضُهُمْ ذَاتَ الشِّمَالِ وَهُمْ فِىْ فَجْوَةٍ مِّنْهُ‌ ؕ ذٰ لِكَ مِنْ اٰيٰتِ اللّٰهِ‌ ؕ مَنْ يَّهْدِ اللّٰهُ فَهُوَ الْمُهْتَدِ ‌ۚ وَمَنْ يُّضْلِلْ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهٗ وَلِيًّا مُّرْشِدًا‏(17)\n\nYou may see the sun, when it rises, slanting toward the right of their cave, and, when it sets, cut across them towards the left, and they are in a cavern within it. That is one of Allah’s signs. Whomever Allah guides is rightly guided, and whomever He leads astray, you will never find for him any guardian or guide.\nوَ تَحْسَبُهُمْ اَيْقَاظًا وَّهُمْ رُقُوْدٌ ‌‌ۖ وَنُقَلِّبُهُمْ ذَاتَ الْيَمِيْنِ وَ ذَاتَ الشِّمَالِ‌‌ ۖ وَكَلْبُهُمْ بَاسِطٌ ذِرَاعَيْهِ بِالْوَصِيْدِ‌ ؕ لَوِ اطَّلَعْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ لَوَلَّيْتَ مِنْهُمْ فِرَارًا وَّلَمُلِئْتَ مِنْهُمْ رُعْبًا‏(18)\n\nYou will suppose them to be awake, although they are asleep. We turn them to the right and to the left, and their dog [lies] stretching its forelegs at the threshold. If you come upon them, you will surely turn to flee from them, and you will surely be filled with a terror of them.\nوَكَذٰلِكَ بَعَثْنٰهُمْ لِيَتَسَآءَلُوْا بَيْنَهُمْ‌ ؕ قَالَ قَآئِلٌ مِّنْهُمْ كَمْ لَبِثْتُمْ ؕ قَالُوْا لَبِثْنَا يَوْمًا اَوْ بَعْضَ يَوْمٍ‌ ؕ قَالُوْا رَبُّكُمْ اَعْلَمُ بِمَا لَبِثْتُمْ ؕ فَابْعَثُوْۤا اَحَدَكُمْ بِوَرِقِكُمْ هٰذِهٖۤ اِلَى الْمَدِيْنَةِ فَلْيَنْظُرْ اَيُّهَاۤ اَزْكٰى طَعَامًا فَلْيَاْتِكُمْ بِرِزْقٍ مِّنْهُ وَلْيَتَلَطَّفْ وَلَا يُشْعِرَنَّ بِكُمْ اَحَدًا‏(19)\n\nSo it was that We aroused them [from sleep] so that they might question one another. One of them said, ‘How long have you stayed [here]?’ They said, ‘We have stayed a day, or part of a day.’ They said, ‘Your Lord knows best how long you have stayed. Send one of you to the city with this money. Let him observe which of them has the purest food, and bring you provisions from there. Let him be attentive, and let him not make anyone aware of you.\nاِنَّهُمْ اِنْ يَّظْهَرُوْا عَلَيْكُمْ يَرْجُمُوْكُمْ اَوْ يُعِيْدُوْكُمْ فِىْ مِلَّتِهِمْ وَلَنْ تُفْلِحُوْۤا اِذًا اَبَدًا‏(20)\n\nIndeed should they prevail over you, they will [either] stone you [to death], or force you back into their creed, and then you will never be saved. ’\nوَكَذٰلِكَ اَعْثَرْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ لِيَعْلَمُوْۤا اَنَّ وَعْدَ اللّٰهِ حَقٌّ وَّاَنَّ السَّاعَةَ لَا رَيْبَ فِيْهَا ‌ۚ اِذْ يَتَنَازَعُوْنَ بَيْنَهُمْ اَمْرَهُمْ‌ فَقَالُوْا ابْنُوْا عَلَيْهِمْ بُنْيَانًا ‌ ؕ رَبُّهُمْ اَعْلَمُ بِهِمْ‌ؕ قَالَ الَّذِيْنَ غَلَبُوْا عَلٰٓى اَمْرِهِمْ لَنَتَّخِذَنَّ عَلَيْهِمْ مَّسْجِدًا‏(21)\n\nSo it was that We let them come upon them, that they might know that Allah’s promise is true, and that there is no doubt in the Hour. As they disputed among themselves about their matter, they said, ‘Build a building over them. Their Lord knows them best.’ Those who had the say in their matter said, ‘We will set up a place of worship over them.’\nسَيَقُوْلُوْنَ ثَلٰثَةٌ رَّابِعُهُمْ كَلْبُهُمْ‌ۚ وَيَقُوْلُوْنَ خَمْسَةٌ سَادِسُهُمْ كَلْبُهُمْ رَجْمًۢا بِالْغَيْبِ‌ۚ وَيَقُوْلُوْنَ سَبْعَةٌ وَّثَامِنُهُمْ كَلْبُهُمْ‌ؕ قُلْ رَّبِّىْۤ اَعْلَمُ بِعِدَّتِهِمْ مَّا يَعْلَمُهُمْ اِلَّا قَلِيْلٌ  فَلَا تُمَارِ فِيْهِمْ اِلَّا مِرَآءً ظَاهِرًا وَّلَا تَسْتَفْتِ فِيْهِمْ مِّنْهُمْ اَحَدًا‏(22)\n\nThey will say, ‘[They are] three; their dog is the fourth of them,’ and say, ‘[They are] five, their dog is the sixth of them,’ taking a shot at the invisible. They will say, ‘[They are] seven, their dog is the eighth of them.’ Say, ‘My Lord knows best their number, and none knows them except a few.’ So do not dispute concerning them, except for a seeming dispute, and do not question about them any of them.\nوَلَا تَقُوْلَنَّ لِشَاىْءٍ اِنِّىْ فَاعِلٌ ذٰ لِكَ غَدًا ۙ‏(23)\n\nDo not say about anything, ‘I will indeed do it tomorrow,’\nاِلَّاۤ اَنْ يَّشَآءَ اللّٰهُ‌ وَاذْكُرْ رَّبَّكَ اِذَا نَسِيْتَ وَقُلْ عَسٰٓى اَنْ يَّهْدِيَنِ رَبِّىْ لِاَقْرَبَ مِنْ هٰذَا رَشَدًا‏(24)\n\nwithout [adding], ‘God willing.’ And when you forget, remember your Lord, and say, ‘Maybe my Lord will guide me to [something] more akin to rectitude than this.’\nوَلَبِثُوْا فِىْ كَهْفِهِمْ ثَلٰثَ مِائَةٍ سِنِيْنَ وَازْدَادُوْا تِسْعًا‏(25)\n\nThey remained in the Cave for three hundred years, and added nine more [to that number].\nقُلِ اللّٰهُ اَعْلَمُ بِمَا لَبِثُوْا‌ ۚ لَهٗ غَيْبُ السَّمٰوٰتِ وَالْاَرْضِ‌ ؕ اَبْصِرْ بِهٖ وَاَسْمِعْ‌ ؕ مَا لَهُمْ مِّنْ دُوْنِهٖ مِنْ وَّلِىٍّ  وَّلَا يُشْرِكُ فِىْ حُكْمِهٖۤ اَحَدًا‏(26)\n???\nSay, ‘Allah knows best how long they remained. To Him belongs the Unseen of the heavens and the earth. How well does He see! How well does He hear! They have no guardian besides Him, and none shares with Him in His judgement.’\nوَاتْلُ مَاۤ اُوْحِىَ اِلَيْكَ مِنْ كِتَابِ رَبِّكَ ‌ؕ لَا مُبَدِّلَ لِكَلِمٰتِهٖ‌ ۚ وَلَنْ تَجِدَ مِنْ دُوْنِهٖ مُلْتَحَدًا‏(27)\n???\nRecite what has been revealed to you from the Book of your Lord. Nothing can change His words, and you will never find any refuge besides Him.\nوَاصْبِرْ نَفْسَكَ مَعَ الَّذِيْنَ يَدْعُوْنَ رَبَّهُمْ بِالْغَدٰوةِ وَالْعَشِىِّ يُرِيْدُوْنَ وَجْهَهٗ‌ وَلَا تَعْدُ عَيْنٰكَ عَنْهُمْ‌ ۚ تُرِيْدُ زِيْنَةَ الْحَيٰوةِ الدُّنْيَا‌ ۚ وَ لَا تُطِعْ مَنْ اَغْفَلْنَا قَلْبَهٗ عَنْ ذِكْرِنَا وَاتَّبَعَ هَوٰٮهُ وَكَانَ اَمْرُهٗ فُرُطًا‏(28)\n???\nContent yourself with the company of those who supplicate their Lord morning and evening, desiring His Face, and do not lose sight of them, desiring the glitter of the life of this world. And do not obey him whose heart We have made oblivious to Our remembrance, and who follows his own desires, and whose conduct is [mere] profligacy.\nوَقُلِ الْحَقُّ مِنْ رَّبِّكُمْ‌ فَمَنْ شَآءَ فَلْيُؤْمِنْ وَّمَنْ شَآءَ فَلْيَكْفُرْ ‌ۙاِنَّاۤ اَعْتَدْنَا لِلظّٰلِمِيْنَ نَارًا ۙ اَحَاطَ بِهِمْ سُرَادِقُهَا‌ ؕ وَاِنْ يَّسْتَغِيْثُوْا يُغَاثُوْا بِمَآءٍ كَالْمُهْلِ يَشْوِى الْوُجُوْهَ‌ؕ بِئْسَ الشَّرَابُ وَسَآءَتْ مُرْتَفَقًا‏(29)\n\nAnd say, ‘[This is] the truth from your Lord: let anyone who wishes believe it, and let anyone who wishes disbelieve it.’ Indeed, We have prepared for the wrongdoers a Fire whose curtains will surround them [on all sides]. If they cry out for help, they will be helped with a water like molten copper, which will scald their faces. What an evil drink, and how ill a resting place!\nاِنَّ الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا وَعَمِلُوا الصّٰلِحٰتِ اِنَّا لَا نُضِيْعُ اَجْرَ مَنْ اَحْسَنَ عَمَلًا‌ ۚ‏(30)\n\nAs for those who have faith and do righteous deeds—indeed We do not waste the reward of those who are good in deeds.\nاُولٰۤئِكَ لَهُمْ جَنّٰتُ عَدْنٍ تَجْرِىْ مِنْ تَحْتِهِمُ الْاَنْهٰرُ يُحَلَّوْنَ فِيْهَا مِنْ اَسَاوِرَ مِنْ ذَهَبٍ وَّ يَلْبَسُوْنَ ثِيَابًا خُضْرًا مِّنْ سُنْدُسٍ وَّاِسْتَبْرَقٍ مُّتَّكِئِيْنَ فِيْهَا عَلَى الْاَرَآئِكِ‌ؕ نِعْمَ الثَّوَابُ ؕ وَحَسُنَتْ مُرْتَفَقًا‏(31)\n\nFor such there will be the gardens of Eden with streams running in them. They will be adorned therein with bracelets of gold and wear green garments of silk and brocade, reclining therein on couches. How excellent a reward, and how good a resting place!\nوَاضْرِبْ لَهُمْ مَّثَلًا رَّجُلَيْنِ جَعَلْنَا لِاَحَدِهِمَا جَنَّتَيْنِ مِنْ اَعْنَابٍ وَّحَفَفْنٰهُمَا بِنَخْلٍ وَّجَعَلْنَا بَيْنَهُمَا زَرْعًا ؕ‏(32)\n\nDraw for them the parable of two men for each of whom We had made two gardens of vines, and We had surrounded them with date palms, and placed crops between them.\nكِلْتَا الْجَنَّتَيْنِ اٰتَتْ اُكُلَهَا وَلَمْ تَظْلِمْ مِّنْهُ شَيْئًا‌ ۙ وَّفَجَّرْنَا خِلٰلَهُمَا نَهَرًا ۙ‏(33)\n\nBoth gardens yielded their produce without stinting anything of it. And We had set a stream gushing through them.\nوَكَانَ لَهٗ ثَمَرٌ‌ ۚ فَقَالَ لِصَاحِبِهٖ وَهُوَ يُحَاوِرُهٗۤ اَنَا اَكْثَرُ مِنْكَ مَالًا وَّاَعَزُّ نَفَرًا‏(34)\n\nHe had abundant fruits, so he said to his companion, as he conversed with him: ‘I have more wealth than you, and am stronger with respect to numbers.’\nوَدَخَلَ جَنَّتَهٗ وَهُوَ ظَالِمٌ لِّنَفْسِهٖ‌ ۚ قَالَ مَاۤ اَظُنُّ اَنْ تَبِيْدَ هٰذِهٖۤ اَبَدًا ۙ‏(35)\n\nHe entered his garden while he wronged himself. He said, ‘I do not think that this will ever perish,\nوَّمَاۤ اَظُنُّ السَّاعَةَ قَآئِمَةً  ۙ وَّلَئِنْ رُّدِدْتُّ اِلٰى رَبِّىْ لَاَجِدَنَّ خَيْرًا مِّنْهَا مُنْقَلَبًا‏(36)\n\nand I do not think that the Hour will ever set in. And even if I am returned to my Lord I will surely find a resort better than this.’\nقَالَ لَهٗ صَاحِبُهٗ وَهُوَ يُحَاوِرُهٗۤ اَكَفَرْتَ بِالَّذِىْ خَلَقَكَ مِنْ تُرَابٍ ثُمَّ مِنْ نُّطْفَةٍ ثُمَّ سَوّٰٮكَ رَجُلًاؕ‏(37)\n\nHis companion said to him, as he conversed with him: ‘Do you disbelieve in Him who created you from dust, then from a drop of [seminal] fluid, then fashioned you as a man?\nلّٰكِنَّا۟ هُوَ اللّٰهُ رَبِّىْ وَلَاۤ اُشْرِكُ بِرَبِّىْۤ اَحَدًا‏(38)\n\nBut I [say], ‘‘He is Allah, my Lord,’’ and I do not ascribe any partner to my Lord.\nوَلَوْلَاۤ اِذْ دَخَلْتَ جَنَّتَكَ قُلْتَ مَا شَآءَ اللّٰهُ ۙ لَا قُوَّةَ اِلَّا بِاللّٰهِ‌ ۚ اِنْ تَرَنِ اَنَا اَقَلَّ مِنْكَ مَالًا وَّوَلَدًا‌ ۚ‏(39)\n\nWhy did you not say, when you entered your garden, ‘‘[This is] as Allah has willed! There is no power except by Allah!’’ If you see that I have lesser wealth than you and children,\nفَعَسٰى رَبِّىْۤ اَنْ يُّؤْتِيَنِ خَيْرًا مِّنْ جَنَّتِكَ وَيُرْسِلَ عَلَيْهَا حُسْبَانًا مِّنَ السَّمَآءِ فَتُصْبِحَ صَعِيْدًا زَلَقًا ۙ‏(40)\n\nmaybe my Lord will give me [something] better than your garden, and He will unleash upon it bolts from the sky, so that it becomes a bare plain.\nاَوْ يُصْبِحَ مَآؤُهَا غَوْرًا فَلَنْ تَسْتَطِيْعَ لَهٗ طَلَبًا‏(41)\n\nOr its water will sink down, so that you will never be able to obtain it.’\nوَاُحِيْطَ بِثَمَرِهٖ فَاَصْبَحَ يُقَلِّبُ كَفَّيْهِ عَلَىٰ مَاۤ اَنْفَقَ فِيْهَا وَهِىَ خَاوِيَةٌ عَلٰى عُرُوْشِهَا وَيَقُوْلُ يٰلَيْتَنِىْ لَمْ اُشْرِكْ بِرَبِّىْۤ اَحَدًا‏(42)\n\nAnd ruin closed in on his produce, and he began to wring his hands for what he had spent on it, as it lay fallen on its trellises. He was saying, ‘I wish I had not ascribed any partner to my Lord.’\nوَلَمْ تَكُنْ لَّهٗ فِئَةٌ يَّنْصُرُوْنَهٗ مِنْ دُوْنِ اللّٰهِ وَمَا كَانَ مُنْتَصِرًا ؕ‏(43)\n\nHe had no party to help him, besides Allah, nor could he help himself.\nهُنَالِكَ الْوَلَايَةُ لِلّٰهِ الْحَقِّ‌ؕ هُوَ خَيْرٌ ثَوَابًا وَّخَيْرٌ عُقْبًا‏(44)\n\nThere, all authority belongs to Allah, the Real. He is best in rewarding, and best in requiting.\nوَاضْرِبْ لَهُمْ مَّثَلَ الْحَيٰوةِ الدُّنْيَا كَمَآءٍ اَنْزَلْنٰهُ مِنَ السَّمَآءِ فَاخْتَلَطَ بِهٖ نَبَاتُ الْاَرْضِ فَاَصْبَحَ هَشِيْمًا تَذْرُوْهُ الرِّيٰحُ‌ ؕ وَكَانَ اللّٰهُ عَلٰى كُلِّ شَىْءٍ مُّقْتَدِرًا‏(45)\n\nDraw for them the parable of the life of this world: [It is] like the water We send down from the sky. Then the earth’s vegetation mingles with it. Then it becomes chaff, scattered by the wind. And Allah has power over all things.\nاَلْمَالُ وَ الْبَنُوْنَ زِيْنَةُ الْحَيٰوةِ الدُّنْيَا‌ ۚ وَالْبٰقِيٰتُ الصّٰلِحٰتُ خَيْرٌ عِنْدَ رَبِّكَ ثَوَابًا وَّخَيْرٌ اَمَلًا‏(46)\n\nWealth and children are an adornment of the life of the world, but lasting righteous deeds are better with your Lord in reward and better in hope.\nوَيَوْمَ نُسَيِّرُ الْجِبَالَ و تَرَى الْاَرْضَ بَارِزَةً  ۙ وَّحَشَرْنٰهُمْ فَلَمْ نُغَادِرْ مِنْهُمْ اَحَدًا‌ ۚ‏(47)\n\nThe day We shall set the mountains moving and you will see the earth in full view, We shall muster them, and We will not leave out anyone of them.\nوَعُرِضُوْا عَلٰى رَبِّكَ صَفًّا ؕ لَقَدْ جِئْتُمُوْنَا كَمَا خَلَقْنٰكُمْ اَوَّلَ مَرَّةٍ ۢ  بَلْ زَعَمْتُمْ اَ لَّنْ نَّجْعَلَ لَكُمْ مَّوْعِدًا‏(48)\n\nThey will be presented before your Lord in ranks: ‘Certainly you have come to Us just as We created you the first time. But you maintained that We shall not appoint a tryst for you.’\nوَوُضِعَ الْكِتٰبُ فَتَرَى الْمُجْرِمِيْنَ مُشْفِقِيْنَ مِمَّا فِيْهِ وَ يَقُوْلُوْنَ يٰوَيْلَتَنَا مَالِ هٰذَا الْكِتٰبِ لَا يُغَادِرُ صَغِيْرَةً وَّلَا كَبِيْرَةً اِلَّاۤ اَحْصٰٮهَا‌ ۚ وَوَجَدُوْا مَا عَمِلُوْا حَاضِرًا‌ ؕ وَ لَا يَظْلِمُ رَبُّكَ اَحَدًا‏(49)\n\nThe Book will be set up. Then you will see the guilty apprehensive of what is in it. They will say, ‘Woe to us! What a book is this! It omits nothing, big or small, without enumerating it.’ They will find present whatever they had done, and your Lord does not wrong anyone.\nوَاِذْ قُلْنَا لِلْمَلٰۤئِكَةِ اسْجُدُوْا لِاٰدَمَ فَسَجَدُوْۤا اِلَّاۤ اِبْلِيْسَؕ كَانَ مِنَ الْجِنِّ فَفَسَقَ عَنْ اَمْرِ رَبِّهٖؕ اَفَتَتَّخِذُوْنَهٗ وَذُرِّيَّتَهٗۤ اَوْلِيَآءَ مِنْ دُوْنِىْ وَهُمْ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّ ؕ بِئْسَ لِلظّٰلِمِيْنَ بَدَلًا‏(50)\n\nWhen We said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’ they prostrated, but not Iblis. He was one of the jinn, so he transgressed against his Lord’s command. Will you then take him and his offspring for guardians in My stead, though they are your enemies? How evil a substitute for the wrongdoers!\nمَّاۤ اَشْهَدْتُّهُمْ خَلْقَ السَّمٰوٰتِ وَالْاَرْضِ وَلَا خَلْقَ اَنْفُسِهِمْ وَمَا كُنْتُ مُتَّخِذَ الْمُضِلِّيْنَ عَضُدًا‏(51)\n\nI did not make them witness to the creation of the heavens and the earth, nor to their own creation, nor do I take those who mislead others as assistants.\nوَيَوْمَ يَقُوْلُ نَادُوْا شُرَكَآءِىَ الَّذِيْنَ زَعَمْتُمْ فَدَعَوْهُمْ فَلَمْ يَسْتَجِيْبُوْا لَهُمْ وَجَعَلْنَا بَيْنَهُمْ مَّوْبِقًا‏(52)\n\nThe day He will say [to the polytheists], ‘Call those whom you maintained to be My partners,’ they will call them, but they will not respond to them, for We shall set an abyss between them.\nوَرَاَ الْمُجْرِمُوْنَ النَّارَ فَظَنُّوْۤا اَنَّهُمْ مُّوَاقِعُوْهَا وَ لَمْ يَجِدُوْا عَنْهَا مَصْرِفًا‏(53)\n\nThe guilty will sight the Fire and know that they are about to fall in it, for they will find no way to escape it.\nوَلَقَدْ صَرَّفْنَا فِىْ هٰذَا الْقُرْاٰنِ لِلنَّاسِ مِنْ كُلِّ مَثَلٍ‌ ؕ وَكَانَ الْاِنْسَانُ اَكْثَرَ شَىْءٍ جَدَلًا‏(54)\n\nWe have certainly interspersed this Quran with every kind of parable for the people. But man is the most disputatious of creatures.\nوَمَا مَنَعَ النَّاسَ اَنْ يُّؤْمِنُوْۤا اِذْ جَآءَهُمُ الْهُدٰى وَيَسْتَغْفِرُوْا رَبَّهُمْ اِلَّاۤ اَنْ تَاْتِيَهُمْ سُنَّةُ الْاَوَّلِيْنَ اَوْ يَاْتِيَهُمُ الْعَذَابُ قُبُلًا‏(55)\n\nNothing has kept these people from believing and pleading to their Lord for forgiveness when guidance came to them, except [their demand] that the precedent of the ancients come to pass for them, or that the punishment come to them, face to face.\nوَمَا نُرْسِلُ الْمُرْسَلِيْنَ اِلَّا مُبَشِّرِيْنَ وَمُنْذِرِيْنَ‌ ۚ وَيُجَادِلُ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا بِالْبَاطِلِ لِيُدْحِضُوْا بِهِ الْحَقَّ‌ وَاتَّخَذُوْۤا اٰيٰتِىْ وَمَاۤ اُنْذِرُوْا هُزُوًا‏(56)\n\nWe do not send the apostles except as bearers of good news and as warners, but those who are faithless dispute fallaciously to refute thereby the truth, having taken My signs and what they are warned of in derision.\nوَمَنْ اَظْلَمُ مِمَّنْ ذُكِّرَ بِاٰيٰتِ رَبِّهٖ فَاَعْرَضَ عَنْهَا وَنَسِىَ مَا قَدَّمَتْ يَدٰهُ‌ ؕ اِنَّا جَعَلْنَا عَلٰى قُلُوْبِهِمْ اَكِنَّةً اَنْ يَّفْقَهُوْهُ وَفِىْۤ اٰذَانِهِمْ وَقْرًا‌ ؕ وَاِنْ تَدْعُهُمْ اِلَى الْهُدٰى فَلَنْ يَّهْتَدُوْۤا اِذًا اَبَدًا‏(57)\n\nWho is a greater wrongdoer than he who is reminded of the signs of his Lord, whereat he disregards them and forgets what his hands have sent ahead? Indeed We have cast veils on their hearts lest they should understand it, and a deafness into their ears; and if you invite them to guidance they will never [let themselves] be guided.\nوَرَبُّكَ الْغَفُوْرُ ذُوْ الرَّحْمَةِ‌ ؕ لَوْ يُؤَاخِذُهُمْ بِمَا كَسَبُوْا لَعَجَّلَ لَهُمُ الْعَذَابَ‌ ؕ بَلْ لَّهُمْ مَّوْعِدٌ لَّنْ يَّجِدُوْا مِنْ دُوْنِهٖ مَوْئِلًا‏(58)\n\nYour Lord is the All-forgiving dispenser of mercy. Were He to take them to task because of what they have committed, He would have surely hastened their punishment. But they have a tryst, [when] they will not find a refuge besides Him.\nوَتِلْكَ الْقُرٰٓى اَهْلَكْنٰهُمْ لَمَّا ظَلَمُوْا وَجَعَلْنَا لِمَهْلِكِهِمْ مَّوْعِدًا‏(59)\n\nThose are the towns that We destroyed when they were wrongdoers, and We appointed a tryst for their destruction.\nوَاِذْ قَالَ مُوْسٰى لِفَتٰٮهُ لَاۤ اَبْرَحُ حَتّٰۤى اَبْلُغَ مَجْمَعَ الْبَحْرَيْنِ اَوْ اَمْضِىَ حُقُبًا‏(60)\n\nWhen Moses said to his lad, ‘I will go on [journeying] until I have reached the confluence of the two seas, or have spent a long time [travelling].’\nفَلَمَّا بَلَغَا مَجْمَعَ بَيْنِهِمَا نَسِيَا حُوْتَهُمَا فَاتَّخَذَ سَبِيْلَهٗ فِى الْبَحْرِ سَرَبًا‏(61)\n\nSo when they reached the confluence between them, they forgot their fish, which found its way into the sea, sneaking away.\nفَلَمَّا جَاوَزَا قَالَ لِفَتٰٮهُ اٰتِنَا غَدَآءَنَا لَقَدْ لَقِيْنَا مِنْ سَفَرِنَا هٰذَا نَصَبًا‏(62)\n\nSo when they had passed on, he said to his lad, ‘Bring us our meal. We have certainly encountered much fatigue on this journey of ours.’\nقَالَ اَرَءَيْتَ اِذْ اَوَيْنَاۤ اِلَى الصَّخْرَةِ فَاِنِّىْ نَسِيْتُ الْحُوْتَ وَ مَاۤ اَنْسٰٮنِيْهُ اِلَّا الشَّيْطٰنُ اَنْ اَذْكُرَهٗ‌ ‌ۚ وَاتَّخَذَ سَبِيْلَهٗ فِىْ الْبَحْر‌ِ ‌ۖ عَجَبًا‏(63)\n\nHe said, ‘Did you see?! When we took shelter at the rock, indeed I forgot about the fish—and none but Satan made me forget to mention it!—and it made its way into the sea in an amazing manner!’\nقَالَ ذٰ لِكَ مَا كُنَّا نَبْغِ ‌‌ۖ  فَارْتَدَّا عَلٰٓى اٰثَارِهِمَا قَصَصًا ۙ‏(64)\n\nHe said, ‘That is what we were after!’ So they returned, retracing their footsteps.\nفَوَجَدَا عَبْدًا مِّنْ عِبَادِنَاۤ اٰتَيْنٰهُ رَحْمَةً مِّنْ عِنْدِنَا وَعَلَّمْنٰهُ مِنْ لَّدُنَّا عِلْمًا‏(65)\n\n[There] they found one of Our servants whom We had granted a mercy from Ourselves, and taught him a knowledge from Our own.\nقَالَ لَهٗ مُوْسٰى هَلْ اَتَّبِعُكَ عَلٰٓى اَنْ تُعَلِّمَنِ مِمَّا عُلِّمْتَ رُشْدًا‏(66)\n\nMoses said to him, ‘May I follow you for the purpose that you teach me some of the probity you have been taught?’\nقَالَ اِنَّكَ لَنْ تَسْتَطِيْعَ مَعِىَ صَبْرًا‏(67)\n\nHe said, ‘Indeed you cannot have patience with me!\nوَكَيْفَ تَصْبِرُ عَلٰى مَا لَمْ تُحِطْ بِهٖ خُبْرًا‏(68)\n\nAnd how can you have patience about something you do not comprehend?’\nقَالَ سَتَجِدُنِىْۤ اِنْ شَآءَ اللّٰهُ صَابِرًا وَّلَاۤ اَعْصِىْ لَكَ اَمْرًا‏(69)\n\nHe said, ‘You will find me, God willing, to be patient, and I will not disobey you in any matter.’\nقَالَ فَاِنِ اتَّبَعْتَنِىْ فَلَا تَسْئَلْنِىْ عَنْ شَىْءٍ حَتّٰٓى اُحْدِثَ لَكَ مِنْهُ ذِكْرًا‏(70)\n\nHe said, ‘If you follow me, do not question me concerning anything until I myself first mention it for you.’\nفَانْطَلَقَا حَتّٰۤى اِذَا رَكِبَا فِى السَّفِيْنَةِ خَرَقَهَا‌ ؕ قَالَ اَخَرَقْتَهَا لِتُغْرِقَ اَهْلَهَا‌ ۚ لَقَدْ جِئْتَ شَيْئًا اِمْرًا‏(71)\n\nSo they went on and when they boarded the boat, he made a hole in it. He said, ‘Did you make a hole in it to drown its people? You have certainly done a monstrous thing!’\nقَالَ اَلَمْ اَقُلْ اِنَّكَ لَنْ تَسْتَطِيْعَ مَعِىَ صَبْرًا‏(72)\n\nHe said, ‘Did I not say that you cannot have patience with me?’\nقَالَ لَا تُؤَاخِذْنِىْ بِمَا نَسِيْتُ وَلَا تُرْهِقْنِىْ مِنْ اَمْرِىْ عُسْرًا‏(73)\n\nHe said, ‘Do not take me to task for my forgetting, and do not be hard upon me.’\nفَانْطَلَقَاحَتّٰۤى اِذَا لَقِيَا غُلٰمًا فَقَتَلَهٗ ۙ قَالَ اَقَتَلْتَ نَفْسًا زَكِيَّةً ۢ بِغَيْرِ نَفْسٍ ؕ لَقَدْ جِئْتَ شَيْئًا نُّكْرًا‏(74)\n\nSo they went on until they came upon a boy, whereat he slew him. He said, ‘Did you slay an innocent soul, without [his having slain] anyone? You have certainly done a dire thing!’\nقَالَ اَ لَمْ اَ قُلْ لَّكَ اِنَّكَ لَنْ تَسْتَطِيْعَ مَعِىَ صَبْرًا‏(75)\nQAALA ALAM AQUL LAKA INNAKA LAN TASTATEE-A’ MA-E’YA SABRAN. \nHe said, ‘Did I not tell you that you cannot have patience with me?’\nقَالَ اِنْ سَاَ لْتُكَ عَنْ شَىْءٍۢ بَعْدَهَا فَلَا تُصٰحِبْنِىْ‌ ۚ قَدْ بَلَغْتَ مِنْ لَّدُنِّىْ عُذْرًا‏(76)\nQAALA IN SA-ALTOKA A’N SHAY-IM BA’-DAHAA FALAA TOSAHEBNEE QAD BALAGHTA MIL LADUNNEE U’ZRAN. \nHe said, ‘If I question you about anything after this, do not keep me in your company. You already have enough excuse on my part.’\nفَانْطَلَقَا حَتّٰۤى اِذَاۤ اَتَيَاۤ اَهْلَ قَرْيَةِ  ۨاسْتَطْعَمَاۤ اَهْلَهَا فَاَبَوْا اَنْ يُّضَيِّفُوْهُمَا فَوَجَدَا فِيْهَا جِدَارًا يُّرِيْدُ اَنْ يَّنْقَضَّ فَاَقَامَهٗ‌ ؕ قَالَ لَوْ شِئْتَ لَتَّخَذْتَ عَلَيْهِ اَجْرًا‏(77)\nFANTALAQAA HATTAA EZAA ATAYAA AHLA QARYATENIS TATA’-MAA AHLAHAA FA-ABAW ANY YOZAYYEFOOHOMAA FA-WAJADAA FEEHAA JEDAARANY YOREEDO ANY YANQAZZA FA-AQAAMAH, QAALA LAW SHE-TA LATTAKHAZTA A’LAYHE AJRAN. \nSo they went on until they came to the people of a town. They asked its people for food, but they refused to extend them any hospitality. There they found a wall which was about to collapse, so he erected it. He said, ‘Had you wished, you could have taken a wage for it.’\nقَالَ هٰذَا فِرَاقُ بَيْنِىْ وَبَيْنِكَ‌‌ ۚ سَاُنَبِّئُكَ بِتَاْوِيْلِ مَا لَمْ تَسْتَطِعْ عَّلَيْهِ صَبْرًا‏(78)\nQAALA HAAZAA FERAAQO BAYNEE WA BAYNEKA, SA-ONABBE-OKA BE-TAA-WEELE MAA LAM TASTATE’ A’LAYHE SABRAN. \nHe said, ‘This is where you and I shall part. I will inform you about the interpretation of that over which you could not maintain patience.\nاَمَّا السَّفِيْنَةُ فَكَانَتْ لِمَسٰكِيْنَ يَعْمَلُوْنَ فِى الْبَحْرِ فَاَرَدْتُّ اَنْ اَعِيْبَهَا وَكَانَ وَرَآءَهُمْ مَّلِكٌ يَّاْخُذُ كُلَّ سَفِيْنَةٍ غَصْبًا‏(79)\nAMMAS SAFEENATO FA-KAANAT LEMASAAKEENA YA’-MALOONA FIL BAHRE FA-ARADTO AN A-E’EBAHAA WA KAANA WARAAA-AHOM MALEKUY YAA-KHOZO KULLA SAFEENATIN GHASBAA.\nAs for the boat, it belonged to some poor people who work on the sea. I wanted to make it defective, for behind them was a king seizing every ship usurpingly.\nوَاَمَّا الْغُلٰمُ فَكَانَ اَبَوٰهُ مُؤْمِنَيْنِ فَخَشِيْنَاۤ اَنْ يُّرْهِقَهُمَا طُغْيَانًا وَّكُفْرًا‌ۚ‏(80)\nWA AMMAL GHOLAAMO FA-KAANA ABAWAAHO MO-MENAYNE FA-KHASHEENAA AN YURHEQAHOMAA TUGHYAANAW WA KUFRAN. \nAs for the boy, his parents were faithful [persons], and We feared he would overwhelm them with rebellion and unfaith.\nفَاَرَدْنَاۤ اَنْ يُّبْدِلَهُمَا رَبُّهُمَا خَيْرًا مِّنْهُ زَكٰوةً وَّاَقْرَبَ رُحْمًا‏(81)\nFA-ARADNAA AY YUBDELAHOMAA RABBOHOMAA KHAYRAM MINHO ZAKAATANW WA AQRABA ROHMAA. \nSo We desired that their Lord should give them in exchange one better than him in respect of purity and closer in mercy.\nوَاَمَّا الْجِدَارُ فَكَانَ لِغُلٰمَيْنِ يَتِيْمَيْنِ فِى الْمَدِيْنَةِ وَكَانَ تَحْتَهٗ كَنْزٌ لَّهُمَا وَكَانَ اَبُوْهُمَا صَالِحًا ۚ فَاَرَادَ رَبُّكَ اَنْ يَّبْلُغَاۤ اَشُدَّهُمَا وَيَسْتَخْرِجَا كَنْزَهُمَا ۖ  رَحْمَةً مِّنْ رَّبِّكَ‌‌ ۚ وَمَا فَعَلْتُهٗ عَنْ اَمْرِىْ‌ ؕ ذٰ لِكَ تَاْوِيْلُ مَا لَمْ تَسْطِعْ عَّلَيْهِ صَبْرًا ؕ‏(82)\nWA AMMAL JEDAARO FA-KAANA LE-GHOLAAMAYNE YATEEMAYNE FIL MEDEENATE WA KAANA TAHTAHU KANZUL LAHOMAA WA KAANA ABOOHOMAA SAALEHAN FA-ARAADA RABBOKA AY YABLOGHAA ASHUDDAHOMAA WA YASTAKHREJAA KANZAHOMAA RAHMATAM MIR RABBEKA WA MAA FA-A’LTOHU A’N AMREE ZAALEKA TAA-WEELO MAA LAM TASTE’ A’LAYHE SABRAA. \nAs for the wall, it belonged to two boy orphans in the city. Under it there was a treasure belonging to them. Their father had been a righteous man. So your Lord desired that they should come of age and take out their treasure—as a mercy from your Lord. I did not do that out of my own accord. This is the interpretation of that over which you could not maintain patience.’\nوَيَسْئَلُوْنَكَ عَنْ ذِى الْقَرْنَيْنِ‌ ؕ قُلْ سَاَ تْلُوْا عَلَيْكُمْ مِّنْهُ ذِكْرًا ؕ‏(83)\nWA YAS-ALOONAKA A’N ZIL QARNAYNE, QUL SA-ATLOO A’LAYKUM MINHO ZIKRAA. \nThey question you concerning Dhul Qarnayn. Say, ‘I will relate to you an account of him.’\nاِنَّا مَكَّنَّا لَهٗ فِى الْاَرْضِ وَاٰتَيْنٰهُ مِنْ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ سَبَبًا ۙ‏(84)\nINNAA MAKKANNAA LAHU FIL ARZE WA AATAYNAAHO MIN KULLE SHAY-IN SABABAA. \nIndeed We had granted him power in the land and given him the means to all things.\nفَاَ تْبَعَ سَبَبًا‏(85)\nFA-ATBA-A’ SABABAA. \nSo he directed a means.\nحَتّٰٓى اِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ الشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِىْ عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍ وَّوَجَدَ عِنْدَهَا قَوْمًا ؕ ‌قُلْنَا يٰذَا الْقَرْنَيْنِ اِمَّاۤ اَنْ تُعَذِّبَ وَاِمَّاۤ اَنْ تَتَّخِذَ فِيْهِمْ حُسْنًا‏(86)\nHATTAA EZAA BALAGHA MAGHREBASH SHAMSE WAJADAHAA TAGHROBO FEE A’YNIN HAME-ATIW WA WAJADA I’NDAHAA QAWMAA, QULNAA YAA ZAL QARNAYNE IMMAA AN TO-A’ZZEBA WA IMMAA AN TATTAKHEZA FEEHIM HUSNAA. \nWhen he reached the place where the sun sets, he found it setting over a warm sea, and by it he found a people. We said, ‘O Dhul Qarnayn! You will either punish them, or treat them with kindness.’\nقَالَ اَمَّا مَنْ ظَلَمَ فَسَوْفَ نُعَذِّبُهٗ ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ اِلٰى رَبِّهٖ فَيُعَذِّبُهٗ عَذَابًا نُّكْرًا‏(87)\nQAALA AMMAA MAN ZALAMA FA-SAWFA NO-A’ZZEBOHU SUMMA YORADDO ELAA RABBEHI FA-YO-A’ZZEBOHU A’ZAABAN NUKRAA. \nHe said, ‘As for him who is a wrongdoer, we will punish him. Then he shall be returned to his Lord and He will punish him with a dire punishment.\nوَاَمَّا مَنْ اٰمَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَالِحًا فَلَهٗ جَزَآءَ  ۨالْحُسْنٰى‌ ۚ وَسَنَقُوْلُ لَهٗ مِنْ اَمْرِنَا يُسْرًا ؕ‏(88)\nWA AMMAA MAN AAMANA WA A’MELA SAALEHAN FA-LAHU JAZAAA-A NIL HUSNAA, WA SANAQOOLO LAHU MIN AMRENAA YUSRAA. \nBut as for him who has faith and acts righteously, he shall have the best reward, and we will assign him easy tasks under our command.’\nثُمَّ اَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا‏(89)\nSUMMA ATBA-A’ SABABAA. \nThereafter he directed another means.\nحَتّٰٓى اِذَابَلَغَ مَطْلِعَ الشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَطْلُعُ عَلٰى قَوْمٍ لَّمْ نَجْعَلْ لَّهُمْ مِّنْ دُوْنِهَا سِتْرًا ۙ‏(90)\nHATTAA EZAA BALAGHA MATLE-A’SH SHAMSE WAJADAHAA TATLO-O’ A’LAA QAWMIL LAM NAJ-A’L LAHUM MIN DOONEHAA SITRAA. \nWhen he reached the place where the sun rises, he found it rising on a people for whom We had not provided any shield against it.\nكَذٰلِكَؕ وَقَدْ اَحَطْنَا بِمَا لَدَيْهِ خُبْرًا‏(91)\nKAZAALEKA WA QAD AHATNAA BEMAA LADAYHE KHUBRAA. \nSo it was, and We were fully aware of whatever [means] he possessed.\nثُمَّ اَتْبَعَ سَبَبًا‏(92)\nSUMMA ATBA-A’ SABABAA. \nThereafter he directed another means.\nحَتّٰٓى اِذَا بَلَغَ بَيْنَ السَّدَّيْنِ وَجَدَ مِنْ دُوْنِهِمَا قَوْمًا ۙ لَّا يَكَادُوْنَ يَفْقَهُوْنَ قَوْلًا‏(93)\nHATTAA EZAA BALAGHA BAYNAS SADDAYNE WAJADA MIN DOONEHEMAA QAWMAL LAA YAKAADOONA YAFQAHOONA QAWLAA.\nWhen he reached [the place] between the two barriers, he found between them a people who could hardly understand a word [of his language].\nقَالُوْا يٰذَا الْقَرْنَيْنِ اِنَّ يَاْجُوْجَ وَمَاْجُوْجَ مُفْسِدُوْنَ فِى الْاَرْضِ فَهَلْ نَجْعَلُ لَكَ خَرْجًا عَلٰٓى اَنْ تَجْعَلَ بَيْنَنَا وَبَيْنَهُمْ سَدًّا‏(94)\nQAALOO YAA ZAL QARNAYNE INNA YAA-JOOJA WA MAA-JOOJA MUFSEDOONA FIL ARZE FA-HAL NAJ-A’LO LAKA KHARJAN A'LAA AN TAJ-A'LA BAYNANAA WA BAYNAHUM SADDAA.\nThey said, ‘O Dhul Qarnayn! Indeed Gog and Magog are causing disaster in this land. Shall we pay you a tribute on condition that you build a barrier between them and us?’\nقَالَ مَا مَكَّنِّىْ فِيْهِ رَبِّىْ خَيْرٌ فَاَعِيْنُوْنِىْ بِقُوَّةٍ اَجْعَلْ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ رَدْمًا ۙ‏(95)\nQAALA MAA MAKKANNEE FEEHE RABBEE KHAYRUN FA-A-E’ENOONEE BE-QUWWATIN AJ-A’L BAYNAKUM WA BAYNAHUM RADMAA.\nHe said, ‘What my Lord has furnished me is better. Yet help me with some strength, and I will make a bulwark between you and them.\nاٰتُوْنِىْ زُبَرَ الْحَدِيْدِ‌ ؕ حَتّٰٓى اِذَا سَاوٰى بَيْنَ الصَّدَفَيْنِ قَالَ انْفُخُوْا‌ ؕ حَتّٰٓى اِذَا جَعَلَهٗ نَارًا ۙ قَالَ اٰتُوْنِىْۤ اُفْرِغْ عَلَيْهِ قِطْرًا ؕ‏(96)\nAATOONEE ZOBARAL HADEEDE HATTAA EZAA SAAWAA BAYNAS SADAFAYNE QAALANFOKHOO, HATTAA EZAA JA-A’LAHU NAARAN QAALA AATOONEE UFRIGH A’LAYHE QITRAA.\nBring me pieces of iron!’ When he had levelled up between the flanks, he said, ‘Blow!’ When he had turned it into fire, he said, ‘Bring me molten copper to pour over it.’\nفَمَا اسْطَاعُوْۤا اَنْ يَّظْهَرُوْهُ وَمَا اسْتَطَاعُوْا لَهٗ نَقْبًا‏(97)\nFAMAS-TAA-O’O ANY YAZHAROOHO WA MASTA-TAA-O’O LAHU NAQBAA.\nSo they could neither scale it, nor could they make a hole in it.\nقَالَ هٰذَا رَحْمَةٌ مِّنْ رَّبِّىْ‌ ۚ فَاِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّىْ جَعَلَهٗ دَكَّآءَ‌ ۚ وَكَانَ وَعْدُ رَبِّىْ حَقًّا ؕ‏(98)\nQAALA HAAZAA RAHMATUM MIR RABBEE FA-EZAA JAAA-A WA’-DO RABBEE JA-A’LAHU DAKKAAA-A, WA KAANA WA’-DO RABBEE HAQQAA.\nHe said, ‘This is a mercy from my Lord. But when the promise of my Lord is fulfilled, He will level it; and my Lord’s promise is true.’\nوَتَرَكْنَا بَعْضَهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍ يَّمُوْجُ فِىْ بَعْضٍ‌ وَّنُفِخَ فِى الصُّوْرِ فَجَمَعْنٰهُمْ جَمْعًا ۙ‏(99)\nWA TARAKNAA BA’ZAHUM YAWMA-EZINY YAMOOJO FEE BA’ZINW WA NOFEKHA FIS SOORE FAJAMA’NAAHUM JAM-A’NW.\nThat day We shall let them surge over one another, the Trumpet will be blown, and We shall gather them all,\nوَّعَرَضْنَا جَهَنَّمَ يَوْمَئِذٍ لِّلْكٰفِرِيْنَ عَرْضَا ۙ‏(100)\nWA A’RAZNAA JAHANNAMA YAWMA-EZIL LIL-KAAFEREENA A’RZAANIL.\nand on that day We shall bring hell into view visibly for the faithless.\nاۨلَّذِيْنَ كَانَتْ اَعْيُنُهُمْ فِىْ غِطَآءٍ عَنْ ذِكْرِىْ وَكَانُوْا لَا يَسْتَطِيْعُوْنَ سَمْعًا‏(101)\nLAZEENA KAANAT A’YONOHUM FEE GHETAAA-IN A’N ZIKREE WA KAANOO LAA YASTATEE-O’ONA SAM-A’A.\n—Those whose eyes were blind to My remembrance and who could not hear.\nاَفَحَسِبَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْۤا اَنْ يَّتَّخِذُوْا عِبَادِىْ مِنْ دُوْنِىْۤ اَوْلِيَآءَ‌ ؕ اِنَّاۤ اَعْتَدْنَا جَهَنَّمَ لِلْكٰفِرِيْنَ نُزُلًا‏(102)\nA-FA-HASEBAL LAZEENA KAFAROO ANY YATTAKHEZOO E’BAADEE MIN DOONEE AWLEYAAA-A, INNAA A-A’TADNAA JAHANNAMA LIL-KAAFEREENA NOZOLAA.\nDo the faithless suppose that they have taken My servants for guardians in My stead? Indeed, We have prepared hell for the hospitality of the faithless.\nقُلْ هَلْ نُنَبِّئُكُمْ بِالْاَخْسَرِيْنَ اَعْمَالًا ؕ‏(103)\nQUL HAL NONABBE-OKOM BIL-AKHSAREENA A-A’MAALAA.\nSay, ‘Shall we inform you who are the biggest losers in their works?\nاَ لَّذِيْنَ ضَلَّ سَعْيُهُمْ فِى الْحَيٰوةِ الدُّنْيَا وَهُمْ يَحْسَبُوْنَ اَنَّهُمْ يُحْسِنُوْنَ صُنْعًا‏(104)\nALLAZEENA ZALLA SA’-YOHUM FIL HAYAATID DUNYAA WA HUM YAHSABOONA ANNAHUM YOHSENOONA SUN-A’A.\nThose whose efforts are misguided in the life of the world, while they suppose they are doing good.’\nاُولٰۤئِكَ الَّذِيْنَ كَفَرُوْا بِاٰيٰتِ رَبِّهِمْ وَلِقَآئِهٖ فَحَبِطَتْ اَعْمَالُهُمْ فَلَا نُقِيْمُ لَهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيٰمَةِ وَزْنًا‏(105)\nOLAAA-EKAL LAZEENA KAFAROO BE-AAYAATE RABBEHIM WA LEQAAA-EHI FA-HABETAT A-A’MAALOHUM FALAA NOQEEMO LAHUM YAWMAL QEYAAMATE WAZNAA.\nThey are the ones who deny the signs of their Lord and encounter with Him. So their works have failed. On the Day of Resurrection We will not give them any weight.\nذٰلِكَ جَزَآؤُهُمْ جَهَنَّمُ بِمَا كَفَرُوْا وَاتَّخَذُوْۤا اٰيٰتِىْ وَرُسُلِىْ هُزُوًا‏(106)\nZAALEKA JAZAAA-OHUM JAHANNAMO BEMAA KAFAROO WAT-TAKHAZOO AAYAATEE WA ROSOLEE HOZOWAA.\nThat is their requital—hell—because of their unfaith and for deriding My signs and apostles.\nاِنَّ الَّذِيْنَ اٰمَنُوْا وَعَمِلُوا الصّٰلِحٰتِ كَانَتْ لَهُمْ جَنّٰتُ الْفِرْدَوْسِ نُزُلًا ۙ‏(107)\nINNAL LAZEENA AAMANOO WA A’MELUS SAALEHAATE KAANAT LAHUM JANNAATUL FIRDAWSE NOZOLAA.\nAs for those who have faith and do righteous deeds, they shall have the gardens of Firdaws for abode,\nخٰلِدِيْنَ فِيْهَا لَا يَبْغُوْنَ عَنْهَا حِوَلًا‏(108)\nKHAALEDEENA FEEHAA LAA YABGHOONA A’NHAA HEWALAA.\nto remain in them [forever]; they will not seek to leave it for another place.\nقُلْ لَّوْ كَانَ الْبَحْرُ مِدَادًا لِّكَلِمٰتِ رَبِّىْ لَنَفِدَ الْبَحْرُ قَبْلَ اَنْ تَنْفَدَ كَلِمٰتُ رَبِّىْ وَلَوْ جِئْنَا بِمِثْلِهٖ مَدَدًا‏(109)\nQUL LAW KAANAL BAHRO MEDAADAL LE-KALEMAATE RABBEE LA-NAFEDAL BAHRO QABLA AN TANFADA KALEMAATO RABBEE WA LAW JE-NAA BE-MISHLEHI MADADAA.\nSay, ‘If the sea were ink for the words of my Lord, the sea would be spent before the words of my Lord are finished, though We replenish it with another like it.’\nقُلْ اِنَّمَاۤ اَنَا بَشَرٌ مِّثْلُكُمْ يُوْحٰٓى اِلَىَّ اَنَّمَاۤ اِلٰهُكُمْ اِلٰهٌ وَّاحِدٌ‌  ۚ فَمَنْ كَانَ يَرْجُوْالِقَآءَ رَبِّهٖ فَلْيَعْمَلْ عَمَلًا صَالِحًاوَّلَايُشْرِكْ بِعِبَادَةِ رَبِّهٖۤ اَحَدًا‏(110)\nQUL INNAMAA ANAA BASHARUM MISLOKUM YOOHAA ELAYYA ANNAMAA ELAAHOKUM ELAAHUNW WAAHEDUN FAMAN KAANA YARJOO LEQAAA-A RABBEHI FAL-YA’-MAL A’MALAN SAALEHANW WA LAA YUSHRIK BE-E’BAADATE RABBEHI AHADAA.\nSay, ‘I am just a human being like you. It has been revealed to me that your God is the One God. So whoever expects to encounter his Lord—let him act righteously, and not associate anyone with the worship of his Lord.’",
  "merits": "This Surah has 110 verses and it was revealed in Makkah. The Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) has said that whoever recites this Surah will be protected from fitna (evil) for eight days. If a person recites the last verse of Surah al-Kahf before sleeping at night, Allah (s.w.t.) creates a noor (light) from his sleeping place up to the Holy Ka’bah and in this light are angels who continually pray for the reciter until he wakes up.\nIt is narrated from Imam Ja’far as-Sadiq (a.s.) that if a person wishes to wake up at a particular time, he should recite the last ayah of this Surah and then make the intention that he will wake up at a certain time and he will wake up at that time. He (a.s.) also said that if a person recites this Surah every Thursday night, he will die the death of a martyr. Keeping this Surah (written) in the house becomes a means of protection from poverty and debts."
}
